# Hot Pot

## Infobox

| Attribute | Value |
|-----------|-------|
| **Cuisine** | Chinese (regional variations) |
| **Style** | Communal, interactive |
| **Duration** | 1.5-2+ hours |
| **Heat Level** | Variable (mild to very spicy) |
| **Related Concepts** | [[Chinese Cuisine/Concepts/sichuan-cuisine]] |
| **Practice** | Host hot pot dinner, master dipping sauces |

## Overview

Hot pot (火锅, Huǒguō) literally means "fire pot" - a communal dining experience where diners cook raw ingredients in simmering broth at the table. A social, interactive meal that spans Chinese regional cuisines, hot pot emphasizes fresh ingredients, dipping sauces, and the joy of cooking together.

## History

**Origins:**
- Northern China (Mongolian influence)
- Portable cooking for nomadic tribes
- Evolved into elaborate tradition
- Spread throughout China

**Regional evolution:**
- **Beijing/Shanghai** - Mild, meat-focused
- **Sichuan/Chongqing** - Spicy, numbing
- **Cantonese** - Fresh seafood, light broths
- **Yunnan** - Wild mushrooms, herbs
- **Manchurian** - Meat, offal, hearty

## The Experience

### Setting Up

**Equipment:**
- **Hot pot pot** - Electric or gas (portable)
- **Split pot** - Two broths (mild + spicy)
- **Heat source** - Induction or gas burner
- **Strainer ladles** - For scooping cooked items
- **Tongs** - For handling raw meat
- **Individual bowls** - For dipping sauces
- **Small plates** - For raw ingredients

**Table arrangement:**
- Pot in center
- Raw ingredients arranged around pot
- Dipping sauce station nearby
- Paper towels, napkins for cleanup

### Broths (锅底, Guōdǐ)

#### Sichuan Spicy Broth (麻辣锅底, Málà guōdǐ)

**The classic hot pot broth**

**Ingredients:**
- Chili oil (spicy base)
- Sichuan peppercorns (numbing)
- Ginger, garlic, scallions
- Dried chilies
- Beef stock
- Spices: Star anise, cinnamon, cloves

**Characteristics:**
- Very spicy (la)
- Numbing (ma)
- Rich, aromatic
- Red color

**Best for:** Beef, lamb, offal, tofu

#### Mild Broth (清汤, Qīngtāng)

**Gentle, flavorful broth**

**Types:**
- **Beef bone broth** - Rich, savory
- **Chicken broth** - Light, delicate
- **Pork broth** - Sweet, mild
- **Mushroom broth** - Umami-rich

**Ingredients:**
- Bones (beef, chicken, pork)
- Ginger, scallions
- Goji berries
- Dried seafood (optional)

**Best for:** Vegetables, seafood, tofu, mild palates

#### Cantonese Seafood Broth

**Fresh, light broth**

**Ingredients:**
- Fish bones, shrimp shells
- Ginger, scallions
- White wine
- Light soy sauce

**Best for:** Seafood, delicate items

#### Mushroom Broth (菌汤, Jūn tāng)

**Vegetarian, umami-rich**

**Ingredients:**
- Dried shiitake, wood ear, enoki
- Fresh button mushrooms
- Ginger, scallions
- Vegetable stock

**Best for:** Vegetables, tofu, mushrooms

### Ingredients (食材, Shícái)

#### Meats

| Type | Cut | Cooking Time |
|------|-----|--------------|
| **Beef** - Thinly sliced (roll), brisket | 15-30 seconds |
| **Lamb** - Thinly sliced, shoulder | 15-30 seconds |
| **Pork** - Thinly sliced, belly | 15-30 seconds |
---
| **Chicken** - Sliced breast/thigh | 30-45 seconds |
| **Offal** - Tripe, liver, intestine | 1-2 minutes |

#### Seafood

| Type | Prep | Cooking Time |
|------|------|--------------|
| **Shrimp** - Whole or peeled | Shell on or off | 30-60 seconds |
| **Fish fillets** - Thinly sliced | | 30-60 seconds |
| **Squid** - Ring or whole | | 30-45 seconds |
| **Clams/Mussels** - Cleaned | | Until open (1-2 min) |
| **Crab** - Cleaned, cracked | | 2-3 minutes |

#### Tofu & Bean Products

| Type | Characteristics |
|------|----------------|
| **Silken tofu** - Delicate, breaks easily |
| **Soft tofu** - Creamy, soft |
| **Firm tofu** - Holds shape well |
| **Fried tofu** - Golden, crispy exterior |
| **Bean curd sheets** - Layered, absorbent |
| **Fish balls** - Bouncy, mild |
| **Beef balls** - Savory |

#### Vegetables

| Type | Prep | Cooking Time |
|------|------|--------------|
| **Bok choy** - Baby, regular | Whole or halved | 30-60 seconds |
| **Napa cabbage** - Chopped | | 30-60 seconds |
| **Spinach** - Whole | | 20-30 seconds |
| **Chinese broccoli** - Stems | | 45-60 seconds |
| **Corn** - Baby or on cob | | 1-2 minutes |
| **Mushrooms** - Button, shiitake, enoki | Whole or sliced | 30-60 seconds |
| **Lotus root** - Sliced | | 1-2 minutes |
| **Carrots** - Sliced or julienned | | 1-2 minutes |
| **Snow peas** - Whole | | 30 seconds |
| **Bamboo shoots** - Sliced | | 30-60 seconds |

#### Carbohydrates

| Type | Cooking Time |
|------|--------------|
| **Instant noodles** | 1-2 minutes |
| **Fresh noodles** - Udon, ramen | 2-3 minutes |
| **Rice noodles** - Glass (mung bean) | 1-2 minutes |
| **Wontons** | 2-3 minutes |
| **Dumplings** | 2-3 minutes |

#### Other

| Item | Use |
|------|-----|
| **Eggs** - Beaten or whole | Drop into broth |
| **Corn** - On cob or kernels | Natural sweetness |
| **Quail eggs** - Hard-boiled | Protein |

### Dipping Sauces (蘸料, Zhànliào)

#### Classic Sichuan Dipping Sauce

**Spicy, numbing, umami**

**Ingredients:**
- Sesame oil (base)
- Chopped garlic
- Chopped scallions
- Cilantro
- Chili oil (to taste)
- Soy sauce (to taste)
- Vinegar (to taste)

#### Cantonese Dipping Sauce

**Light, aromatic**

**Ingredients:**
- Soy sauce
- Scallions (finely chopped)
- Ginger (finely chopped)
- Chopped cilantro
- Oyster sauce (optional)
- Sesame oil (few drops)

#### Seafood Dipping Sauce

**Complements seafood**

**Ingredients:**
- Soy sauce
- Vinegar
- Ginger
- Chopped scallions
- Chili oil (optional)

#### Custom Dipping Bar

**Set up with:**
- **Sesame oil** - Base
- **Soy sauce** - Light, dark
- **Vinegar** - Black, red, white
- **Chili oil** - Various spice levels
- **Garlic** - Minced
- **Scallions** - Chopped
- **Cilantro** - Chopped
- **Peanut butter** - For nutty sauce
- **Hoisin sauce** - Sweet, thick

**Let guests customize their sauce!**

## Cooking Technique

### General Rules

1. **Wait for boil** - Broth should be bubbling
2. **Add broth** - Keep level high (evaporation)
3. **Cook in batches** - Don't overcrowd
4. **Watch timing** - Overcooked = tough
5. **Skim foam** - Keeps broth clear
6. **Eat immediately** - Best hot

### Order of Cooking

**Start with:**
1. **Broth-enhancing items** - Ginger, scallions (add flavor)
2. **Meats** - Thinly sliced (cook quickly, first to get hot)
3. **Seafood** - Fast cooking
4. **Tofu & bean products** - Absorb flavors
5. **Vegetables** - Quick cooking
6. **Carbohydrates** - Noodles, rice (last, fill up)

### Tips

- **Slice meat paper-thin** - Curls when cooked, cooks instantly
- **Skim broth frequently** - Keeps flavor clean
- **Add water** - Broth evaporates, keep topped up
- **Dip, sauce, eat** - Don't leave food in sauce
- **Eat hot** - Temperature is key

## Hosting Hot Pot

### Planning

**For 4-6 people:**
- **2 broths** - Split pot (spicy + mild)
- **Meat** - 2-3 types (beef, lamb, chicken)
- **Seafood** - 1-2 types (shrimp, fish)
- **Tofu/bean products** - 2-3 types
- **Vegetables** - 3-4 types
- **Carbohydrates** - Noodles, rice
- **Dipping sauces** - Set up bar

### Equipment Check

- **Hot pot pot** - Size for group
- **Split pot** - Two broths
- **Burner** - Induction or gas
- **Extension cord** - If needed
- **Strainer ladles** - 2-3
- **Tongs** - For meat
- **Chopsticks** - Per person
- **Individual bowls** - Per person
- **Small plates** - For raw ingredients
- **Paper towels** - For cleanup

### Preparation (Day of)

**2-3 hours before:**
- Slice meat (keep refrigerated)
- Prep vegetables
- Make dipping sauces
- Set up table

**30 minutes before:**
- Start broths
- Set out ingredients
- Prepare dipping bar

### Menu Planning

**For beginners:**
- Start with 1 broth (mild)
- 1 meat (beef)
- 2 vegetables
- 1 type of tofu
- 1 type of noodle

**For experienced:**
- 2 broths (mild + spicy)
- 2-3 meats
- 3-4 vegetables
- 2-3 types of tofu
- 2 types of noodles
- Dipping sauce bar

## Common Mistakes

| Mistake | How to Fix |
|---------|------------|
| Meat not thin enough | Slice paper-thin, semi-frozen meat helps |
| Broth too salty | Dilute with water |
| Overcooking meat | Cook 15-30 seconds max |
| Not skimming broth | Skim foam frequently |
| Underseasoning broth | Taste and adjust before serving |
| Not replenishing broth | Add water as it evaporates |

## Regional Variations

| Region | Style | Characteristics |
|--------|-------|-----------------|
| **Beijing/Shanghai** - Mild broth, meat-heavy |
| **Sichuan/Chongqing** - Very spicy, numbing |
| **Cantonese** - Light, seafood, delicate |
| **Yunnan** - Mushroom, wild herbs |
| **Manchurian** - Hearty, offal |

## Where to Eat

**In China:**
- **Sichuan/Chongqing** - Authentic, very spicy
- **Beijing** - Mild, meat-focused
- **Shanghai** - Light, delicate

**Internationally:**
- Look for "hot pot" or "huo guo"
- Regional restaurants (Sichuan, Cantonese)
- Hot pot chains (Haidilao - worldwide)

